<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="backhaul"><span aria-hidden="true">02</span>Plan the node connection</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">A secondary node needs a path back to the main network. Wired backhaul uses an existing suitable network connection. Wireless backhaul uses Wi-Fi between nodes. The two arrangements have different constraints, and the equipment documentation determines what combinations are supported.</p>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">A wireless node placed at the edge of a weak area may have the same difficulty as the device it is meant to help. Plan a location where the node can both reach the area of interest and maintain a useful connection back to the main node. Do not assume that products from different systems will coordinate because both use the word “mesh”.</p>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">For a manufacturer example, Google’s <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="https://support.google.com/googlehome/answer/7183150?hl=en">Wi-Fi placement guidance</a> explains why points need a strong connection to one another, while its <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="https://support.google.com/googlenest/answer/7215624?hl=en">wired-backhaul and compatibility guidance</a> distinguishes models with Ethernet ports from wireless-only points. These examples are not a product recommendation; use the instructions for your exact equipment.</p>
{% call note("care", "Take care", "Do not improvise connection work") %}<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">This guide does not provide fixed-wiring, electrical or mains instructions. If a suitable connection path is not already available, record that as a constraint and use the relevant qualified provider or current equipment guidance.</p>{% endcall %}
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="check"><span aria-hidden="true">03</span>Check one change at a time</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Before changing equipment, note the device, room, task, time and connection type. After a change, repeat the same task in the same place where practical. A different result is an observation, not proof that one component caused the original problem.</p>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">For repeated dropouts or uncertain symptoms, use the <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="/guides/wifi-dropouts-diagnosis/">Wi-Fi dropout diagnosis</a> guide. It remains the diagnostic owner; this satellite is about placement planning.</p>
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="next"><span aria-hidden="true">04</span>What to do next</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">If the layout question is still unclear, return to the <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="/guides/need-mesh-wifi-access-point-router-position/">mesh Wi-Fi, access point or router-position guide</a>. If you need to take control of a new system after installation, use <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="/guides/take-ownership-new-home-wifi/">checking and taking ownership of a new home Wi-Fi setup</a> for the handover.</p>
